'''5.''' Not good; defective; worthless; poor; of no value: as, ''bad'' coin; ''bad'' debts; a ''bad'' soil; a ''bad'' crop; a ''bad'' piece of work; ''bad'' health.
{{smaller block|Perjuries are common as ''bad'' pence.
{{right|''Cowper'', Expostulation.}}}}

'''6.''' Incorrect; faulty: as, a ''bad'' aim; ''bad'' English; a ''bad'' pronunciation.
{{smaller block|Call, if you will, ''bad'' rhyming a disease.
{{right|''Pope'', Imit. of Horace, II. ii. 182.}}}}

'''7.''' Not valid; not sound: as, a ''bad'' claim; a ''bad'' plea.
{{smaller block|“You had better get a porter’s knot, and carry trunks.” Nor was the advice ''bad''; for a porter was likely to be as plentifully fed, and as comfortably lodged, as a poet.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Samuel Johnson.}}}}

'''8.''' Unfavorable; unfortunate: as, ''bad'' news; ''bad'' success.
{{smaller block|<poem>Perplex’d and troubled at his ''bad'' success
The tempter stood, nor had what to reply.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. R., iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller|[''Bad'' is the ordinary antithesis of ''good'', in all its senses, whether positively, ‘evil,’ ‘harmful,’ or negatively, ‘not good,’ ‘not satisfactory,’ and whether substantively, ‘being evil,’ or causally, ‘causing harm.’ The senses run into one another, the precise application being determined by the context.]—'''Bad blood, bad conscience,''' etc. See the nouns.—'''Bad form,''' conduct not in accordance with good taste or propriety, or not in keeping with the present conventional usage; slightly vulgar; not very refined. [Slang.]}}
{{smaller block|They are taught that to become emotional or enthusiastic over anything is ''bad form''. ''N. A. Rev.'', CXLII. 621.}}
{{smaller|'''In bad odor.''' See ''odor''.—'''With a bad grace.''' See ''grace''.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' That which is bad. (''a'') A bad condition: as, to go to the ''bad'' (see below). (''b'') A bad thing: as, there are ''bads'' and goods among

readings.c1s2:
them.—'''To the bad.''' {{smaller|(''a'') To ruin, financial or moral: as, he and his affairs soon went ''to the bad.'' (''b'') To the wrong side of the account; in arrear or deficit: as, I am now $100 ''to the bad.''}}

'''bad<sup>2</sup>''' (bad). Preterit of ''bid.''

'''badak-tapa''' (bad′ak-tap′ä̤), ''n.'' [Malay.] The Malay name of the rhinoceros of Sumatra.

'''badaneh''' (ba-dä′ne), ''n.'' The tunic worn by the Egyptian califs, made of the very finest quality of linen. {{smaller|The weight of the garment was only 2 ounces, and it is said to have cost 1,000 dinars (about $2,600).}}

'''baddam''' (bad′a̤m), ''n.'' A species of bitter almond imported into some parts of India from Persia, and used as money, with a value of about half a cent.

'''badder†''' (bad′ėr), ''a.'' Old comparative of ''bad.'' See ''bad''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|Lewed peple . . . demen gladly to the ''badder'' ende.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Squire’s Tale, l. 216.}}
Were it ''badder'', it is not the worst. {{right|''Lyly'', Euphues.}}}}

'''badderlocks''' (bad′ėr-loks), ''n.'' [Supposed, without evidence, to stand for ''Balder’s locks.'' Cf. ''balder-brae.''] A name given in Scotland to the edible seaweed ''Alaria esculenta.'' {{smaller|The plant is olive-green, belonging to the order ''Laminariaceæ'', and has a lanceolate frond borne upon a stipe which is continued into a midrib. The stipe bears ribless leaflets along its sides. Also called ''henware'', and in the Orkney Islands ''honey-ware''; in parts of Ireland, ''murlins.''}}

'''baddest†''' (bad′est), ''a.'' Old superlative of ''bad.'' See ''bad''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|The ''baddest'' among the cardinals is chosen pope.
{{right|''Sir E. Sandys'', State of Religion.}}}}

'''baddish''' (bad′ish), ''a.'' [< ''bad''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] Somewhat bad; of inferior character or quality.
{{smaller block|He wrote ''baddish'' verses. {{right|''Jeffrey.''}}}}

